Grunt wrote:
> The message is generated in a very specific pair of circumstances which
> are currently impossible to achieve.
> * You are rubbing a liquid object on a gray stone - the only liquid
>   objects are currently splashes of venom, which you can only have in
>   wizmode.

      It is possible to find venom in normal mode via bones data.  But
the bones file or its ancestor must have come from wizard mode.

> * The liquid object does not have a certain special "known" flag set.

      That's right, and since it will always be set, that message won't
ever be delivered.

>   This known flag is used exclusively for the Amulet of Yendor and its
>   cheap plastic imitations to distinguish whether or not you have
>   actually identified the object.  All other objects automatically have
>   this flag set to 1 (mkobj.c:385-6).

       That's way off base.  The known flag is used for identification
of
many more types of objects than just those two.  It determines
whether you know a weapon's or armor's enchantment, or a wand's,
tool's, or ring's charge count.  The bit of code you've been fooled by
is used when object amnesia makes you forget the Amulet of Yendor.
Normally you don't forget charge count or enchantment, but in the
Amulet's case you would effectively not have forgotten it at all (aside
from turning it back into a candidate for future identification).
Making
you finally forget it when its name is next formatted for display
probably
produces for some obscure bugs that no one's ever likely to notice.

       Many types of objects don't use the known flag (and have the
oc_uses_known flag in their defintion set to false).  For some reason
I've never understood, the known flag gets forced to true instead of
being ignored for such items.  But a lot of types besides those amulets
do use it, and it will start out as false for them.



